WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:03.557
The churn model scored ninety nine percent in the notebook.

2
00:00:03.577 --> 00:00:05.755
In production, seventy.

3
00:00:05.775 --> 00:00:07.505
The exam loves this story.

4
00:00:07.525 --> 00:00:11.005
Today you learn why it happens, and how to fix it.

5
00:00:11.025 --> 00:00:12.955
First, split your data three ways.

6
00:00:12.975 --> 00:00:14.990
Each part has one job.

7
00:00:15.010 --> 00:00:16.553
Training teaches the weights.

8
00:00:16.573 --> 00:00:18.415
Validation picks the settings.

9
00:00:18.435 --> 00:00:21.275
The test set gives the honest score, once.

10
00:00:21.295 --> 00:00:23.538
For time series, split by time.

11
00:00:23.558 --> 00:00:26.560
A random split lets the model see the future.

12
00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:31.060
And watch for leakage: a feature you only know after the outcome.

13
00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.445
Now compare two scores: training and validation.

14
00:00:34.465 --> 00:00:37.595
High on training, low on validation, is overfitting.

15
00:00:37.615 --> 00:00:39.830
The model memorized the rows.

16
00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.630
Low on both is underfitting.

17
00:00:41.650 --> 00:00:43.130
The model is too simple.

18
00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:46.155
Give it more features or more power.

19
00:00:46.175 --> 00:00:49.065
Four tools stop a model from memorizing.

20
00:00:49.085 --> 00:00:49.878
Dropout.

21
00:00:49.898 --> 00:00:53.725
During training, it switches off random neurons at every step.

22
00:00:53.745 --> 00:00:57.800
No neuron can lean on another, so the network learns general patterns.

23
00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:00.510
At prediction, all neurons are on.

24
00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.847
L one pushes weak features to zero.

25
00:01:02.867 --> 00:01:05.022
L two keeps every weight small.

26
00:01:05.042 --> 00:01:07.720
Early stopping ends at the best epoch.

27
00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:09.920
The trap: more layers or more epochs.

28
00:01:09.940 --> 00:01:12.370
They make overfitting worse.

29
00:01:12.390 --> 00:01:16.080
Next, pick the metric that matches the cost of a mistake.

30
00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.355
If a miss is costly, like a disease, use recall.

31
00:01:19.375 --> 00:01:22.440
If a false alarm is costly, use precision.

32
00:01:22.460 --> 00:01:24.503
Lower the threshold for more recall.

33
00:01:24.523 --> 00:01:26.102
Raise it for more precision.

34
00:01:26.122 --> 00:01:27.675
No retraining.

35
00:01:27.695 --> 00:01:30.387
With rare cases, like fraud, accuracy lies.

36
00:01:30.407 --> 00:01:32.875
Use PR AUC.

37
00:01:32.895 --> 00:01:34.025
Last, features.

38
00:01:34.045 --> 00:01:36.185
They matter more than the algorithm.

39
00:01:36.205 --> 00:01:37.272
Scale your numbers.

40
00:01:37.292 --> 00:01:38.935
One hot small categories.

41
00:01:38.955 --> 00:01:40.645
Embed huge ones.

42
00:01:40.665 --> 00:01:44.980
And cross features that work together, like latitude and longitude.

43
00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:50.330
Put these steps in the transform clause, so prediction repeats them exactly.

44
00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:51.590
Recap.

45
00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:52.677
Split honestly.

46
00:01:52.697 --> 00:01:53.702
Read the gap.

47
00:01:53.722 --> 00:01:55.340
Dropout for big networks.

48
00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:58.000
Pick the metric by the cost of a mistake.

49
00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:00.795
Try the checkpoint below.
